WEBVTT

00:02.070 --> 00:08.040
Beethoven's First Symphony reveals its tonality in a very gradual and very interesting way,

00:08.310 --> 00:16.260
and it's complicated by the fact that, well, Romantic symphonies were literally advertised and published with their key in the title —

00:16.440 --> 00:20.190
and this one is called Symphony #1 in C major.

00:20.550 --> 00:26.820
With that in mind, we might expect the symphony to begin with a C major harmony, but not this one.

00:26.910 --> 00:33.570
It begins on C, all right, but with a B-flat in the chord that changes it to a dominant seventh quality.

00:35.240 --> 00:39.110
It's a little earlier in this course to be talking about chord types like this,

00:39.110 --> 00:45.500
but let's understand that this chord is typically heard as being on the fifth scale degree in F major.

00:45.920 --> 00:52.340
It sets up an expectation that F will be the next chord, and that F is the home key.

00:52.760 --> 00:56.900
And by the end of the bar, our suspicions seem to be confirmed.

01:02.280 --> 01:08.710
But the next bar turns everything on its head. It begins with a chord that isn't in F major at all.

01:08.730 --> 01:17.190
It's AG7 chord. Again, one might expect dominant seventh chords like this to be, well, dominants of things,

01:17.190 --> 01:26.430
which would mean that G7 would be built on the fifth scale degree of C major, which is great because C major is the key advertised!

01:26.940 --> 01:28.890
But then a bit of a fakeout occurs.

01:29.070 --> 01:40.720
The G7 chord progresses to an Am chord, but we're in good territory here, since Am is a typical chord in the key of C major:

01:40.740 --> 01:46.080
it's the chord that's built on the sixth, first, and third scale steps of C.

01:51.660 --> 01:56.220
But for the third bar, another disorienting chord is heard.

01:56.550 --> 02:03.030
The bar starts with a D7 chord, and D7 implies a dominant in the key of G.

02:03.390 --> 02:11.310
And we actually do get a G chord next, which I think feels comforting, like the music has actually arrived at its tonic harmony.

02:19.490 --> 02:27.080
And that's fine with me for now, since I don't have perfect pitch, and so I can't tell that we're actually in the wrong key.

02:28.700 --> 02:37.040
The music continues, and in the fifth bar things get weird again. An F added to the G major chord makes it a dominant seventh,

02:37.490 --> 02:43.490
which seems to be the main device Beethoven was using to guide the tonality in these mysterious ways.

02:44.240 --> 02:53.810
The music continues using just the tones of the key C major, except for a couple of chromatic tones that don't have much of a tonal impact.

02:53.960 --> 03:01.650
And soon the music cadences strongly in the key of C major, just in time for the music pick up tempo and begin in earnest.

03:31.060 --> 03:36.700
There's another famous example of this sort of ambiguity that leads court by court to settle again to a key:

03:36.730 --> 03:43.990
that's the Beatles song "She Loves You." It begins with a non-tonic chord, and then seems to try out various options until it "gets things right."

03:44.470 --> 03:49.960
The music starts with an E minor chord, and for all we know at that point, that's what key the music is in.

03:50.610 --> 04:00.850
You know. And it's a perfect chord for the melody, since the melody features the tones E and G, which are parts of that E minor harmony.

04:01.360 --> 04:04.630
The melody fragment then repeats, this time under an A chord.

04:07.360 --> 04:13.600
Which seems to work nicely too, because those melody tones, E and G are also tones of the A7 chord,

04:13.960 --> 04:18.280
but the melody fragment then repeats again, now harmonized by a C chord.

04:21.600 --> 04:25.410
Which — guess what? — also features the tones E and G!

04:25.680 --> 04:29.610
It isn't until the seventh bar of the song that we get to hear the descending

04:29.790 --> 04:32.790
"yeah yeah yeah" pattern progress one more

04:32.790 --> 04:41.240
"yeah," this time ending on D, which is harmonized by the actual tonic chord of the song.
